{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue 31:\n1. \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n2. \"npm test passes\"\n\nContext: the repo has src/config.js (loadConfig is `return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));`), src/app.js (merges loadConfig's result over DEFAULTS, and on any thrown error prints `bad config: <message>` and exits 1), test/config.test.js (node:test; it currently has a test 'bad JSON throws' that expects a SyntaxError), and README.md (it says invalid JSON stops the app with `bad config: <message>` and exit 1). `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In src/config.js, keep reading the file with readFileSync outside the try, so a missing or unreadable file still throws as it does now. Wrap only the JSON.parse call in try/catch. When parsing fails, return {}.\n2. The issue's real problem is that people can't tell which file is broken or where the error is. Silently returning {} would hide that. So in the catch, before returning {}, write one line to stderr (for example with process.stderr.write or console.error) that names the path and includes the parse error's message, which already gives the position, e.g. `config.json is not valid JSON, using defaults: <error.message>`. Don't print a stack trace. The return value must still be exactly {}.\n3. In test/config.test.js, replace the 'bad JSON throws' test with one that asserts `loadConfig(file('{port: 9000'))` deep-equals {} and doesn't throw. Keep the 'reads a config' test. Optionally add a test that a nonexistent path still throws.\n4. Update README.md so it describes the new behaviour: a file that isn't valid JSON is reported on stderr with its path and the error, and the app starts with the defaults. src/app.js can keep its try/catch for read errors. If its `bad config` wording no longer fits, adjust it, but don't change how valid configs are merged.\n5. Run `npm test` and confirm every test passes. Also run `node src/app.js <file with {port: 9000>` once and check that it prints the stderr line with the path and then `listening on 8080`.\n6. Open the pull request. In its description, quote both acceptance lines and say how each one is met.","verifier":"Check the pull request's head. Do one check per acceptance line, in this order.\n\nCheck 1: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRead src/config.js at the head to confirm that loadConfig catches the error thrown by JSON.parse. Then run it from the repository root against a file with invalid JSON:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/config.json\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig(process.argv[1]); console.log(JSON.stringify(r));\" \"$d/config.json\"`\nPASS: the call doesn't throw and the printed result is `{}` (an empty object).\nFAIL: the call throws (a SyntaxError or anything else), or it returns something other than an empty object. Also FAIL if loadConfig isn't exported from src/config.js.\n\nCheck 2: \"npm test passes\"\nRun `npm test` from the repository root at the head. If the repository has CI checks at the head, you may also read them.\nPASS: `npm test` finishes and reports no failing tests.\nFAIL: any test fails, or `npm test` can't run.\n\nAdvisory (none of this affects PASS or FAIL):\n- The issue says people need to know which file is broken and where. Note whether the catch writes a stderr line that names the path and includes the parse error message, and whether `node src/app.js \"$d/config.json\"` prints that line and then starts with the defaults (`listening on 8080`). If it swallows the error silently, report that as a concern.\n- Note whether a nonexistent path still throws instead of returning {}. Only JSON.parse errors should be caught.\n- Note whether the old 'bad JSON throws' test was replaced by one that asserts {}, rather than just deleted, and whether README.md was updated to match the new behaviour."}